accessWidener v2 named

# Access control for the 1.21.11 target.
#
# Prism's accessWidener(...) in the root build script is the only place this
# is declared; it feeds Fabric as-is and converts the same file into a NeoForge
# access transformer.
#
# The header above is the entire 1.21.11 -> 26.x difference. A 1.21.11 target is
# obfuscated and remapped by fabric-loom-remap, so it needs the "named"
# namespace. A 26.x target ships unobfuscated, where Loom expects "official" --
# which on 26.x is already the Mojang name, so the NeoForge conversion stays
# correct on both.
#
# The header must be line 1 and the separators must be spaces, for the same
# reasons as the 26.1 class tweaker: Loom reads the first line verbatim and
# Prism's converter is more forgiving than Loom's reader.

# --- Phase 2: block entity types -------------------------------------------
# 1.21.11 has no BlockEntityType.Builder either, and its construction routes
# are the same pair 26.1 has: a public static register(String, BlockEntitySupplier,
# Block...) and a private constructor
#
#   private BlockEntityType(BlockEntitySupplier<? extends T>, Set<Block>)
#
# The constructor is what gets widened rather than the static register, for the
# reason spelled out in the 26.x classtweaker: 26.2 deleted register outright,
# so an entry naming it cannot work on every target, whereas the constructor
# exists on all four with the same descriptor -- private on 1.21.11 and 26.1,
# already public on 26.2. Widening it is a no-op where it is already public.
accessible method net/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType <init> (Lnet/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType$BlockEntitySupplier;Ljava/util/Set;)V

# Unlike 26.1 and 26.2, this one is load-bearing here: 1.21.11 declares
# BlockEntitySupplier package-private, so the constructor stays unreachable
# until the type itself is widened. Matches "<T>BlockEntityType(BlockEntitySupplier
# <? extends T>,Set<Block>) has private access" at VanillaRegistrar.
accessible class net/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType$BlockEntitySupplier

# --- Phase 4: menu types ---------------------------------------------------
# The previous revision of this block widened MenuType.register and claimed that
# 1.21.11 had no public construction route. javap -p on this target contradicts
# that, exactly as it does on 26.1:
#
#   public net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType(
#       net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType$MenuSupplier<T>,
#       net.minecraft.world.flag.FeatureFlagSet);
#
#   public interface net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType$MenuSupplier<T ...> {
#       T create(int, Inventory);
#   }
#
# The block had mixed up the public 2-argument register with the private
# 3-argument varargs overload beside it, which is the only private one:
#
#   private static <T ...> MenuType<T> register(String, MenuSupplier<T>, FeatureFlag...);
#
# That mistake is why it "preferred register to the constructor because it fills in
# the FeatureFlagSet itself": register does pass FeatureFlags.VANILLA_SET, but it
# also performs a registry write, through the String overload of
# Registry.register and therefore under the minecraft namespace. Calling it meant
# the type was registered twice -- once as minecraft:carpenter_table_menu by the factory,
# once as decor4fabric:carpenter_table_menu by VanillaRegistrar -- and the second write
# threw "Adding duplicate value ... to registry" during mod init.
#
# So this entry is on the constructor, not on register. Decor4Fabric builds the
# type with "new MenuType<>(supplier, FeatureFlags.VANILLA_SET)" -- the same set
# vanilla's own register passes -- and lets VanillaRegistrar.menuType perform the
# single registry write under the correct key. Widening <init> rather than
# trusting javap's "public" is for the reason given at the BlockEntityType entry
# above: Prism emits a bare "public net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType" line
# for the nested type it widens, and javac then rejects the constructor as private
# until <init> is named.
accessible class net/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType$MenuSupplier
accessible method net/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType <init> (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType$MenuSupplier;Lnet/minecraft/world/flag/FeatureFlagSet;)V

# --- Phase 4: screen registration -------------------------------------------
# 1.21.11 has the same vanilla screen registry the 26.x targets have, with the
# same private entry point and the same package-private functional interface --
# verified with javap -p on this target rather than assumed from 26.1:
#
#   net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens.class
#   net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor.class
#
#   private static <M extends AbstractContainerMenu,
#                   U extends Screen & MenuAccess<M>>
#       void register(MenuType<? extends M>, ScreenConstructor<M, U>);
#     descriptor: (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType;
#                  Lnet/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor;)V
#
# Two entries for the reason given for MenuType above: register is the way in, and
# ScreenConstructor is a parameter type that has to be nameable to pass a lambda
# that returns a screen. An interface, so "accessible class" rather than a method
# entry.
#
# This exists at all because 1.21.11 is late enough that screen registration became
# vanilla API. Earlier targets had no MenuScreens at all, which is why screen
# registration was per-loader then and is not now. What did NOT change with the
# render model: 1.21.11 draws immediately and 26.x extracts, so the two screens
# are separate files even though this registration call is the same line on both.
accessible class net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor
accessible method net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens register (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType;Lnet/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor;)V
